<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capitulary</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as capitularies</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A capitulary was a legislative or administrative ordinance issued by Frankish kings, divided into chapters, or capitula. Capitularies were a principal instrument of government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>, often issued at the annual assembly. They covered matters such as justice, the church, military service and estate management.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capitulary.jpg" alt="Capitulary" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: executed at a scriptorium connected with the court of Charles the Bald · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:9th_century_Beinecke_MS_413_fol._165.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Capitularies came from the Frankish court of both the Merovingian and the Carolingian dynasties, above all that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. The name comes from capitulum, a diminutive of caput, head or heading. Once a capitulary was composed it was sent to the archbishops, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/missi-dominici">missi dominici</a> and counts, and a copy was kept by the chancellor in the palace archives. After the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> they grew long and diffuse, and the last emperor to issue them was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambert-of-italy">Lambert of Italy</a>, in 898. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> also had capitularies, which continued their own laws.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The editor Alfred Boretius divided them into classes. Capitula legibus addenda added to the laws of the peoples, such as the Salic, Ripuarian or Bavarian law, and were promulgated in local assemblies where the people's consent was asked. Capitula ecclesiastica gave force to the canons of councils of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, capitula per se scribenda were political decrees binding on all subjects, and capitula missorum were instructions to the missi on their circuits. Other documents were joined to them, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitulare-de-villis">Capitulare de villis</a> on the royal estates and the partitions of the kingdom in the Divisio regnorum of 806 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ordinatio-imperii">Ordinatio imperii</a> of 817.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>No capitulary survives in its original form; they are known from copies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> going back to the 9th and 10th centuries. In 827 Ansegisus, abbot of St. Wandrille, gathered them in four books, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-pious">Louis the Pious</a> was citing after 829. By 858 a writer calling himself Benedictus Levita had added three more books mixing in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-law">Roman law</a>, church canons and spurious provisions, and modern historians avoid citing them. Étienne Baluze published a new classification in 1677, and the Monumenta Germaniae Historica edition was begun by Boretius in 1883 and continued by Victor Krause.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Capitulary” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>capitulary</b> De Villis, generally attributed to Charlemagne's reign, describes in great detail how a royal estate was expected to function: it specified the crops to be grown, the livestock to be kept, the crafts to be practised and the accounts to be rendered to the king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Charlemagne and Louis the Pious ruled through assemblies, written ordinances called <b>capitularies</b>, travelling royal agents called missi dominici and the network of counts, and tried to bind the realm together through a common Christian discipline, set out in the Admonitio generalis of 789.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Carolingians kept Lombard law and the palace at Pavia but installed Frankish counts and bishops, issued <b>capitularies</b> for Italy and supported monasteries such as Bobbio and Nonantola.</blockquote><a href="/state/carolingian-kingdom-of-italy">Carolingian Kingdom of Italy</a></li></ul></section>
